# Interview Explanation (30–60 sec)

> Since the array is already sorted, I don't need to build the graph explicitly. I only compare adjacent elements. Whenever the difference between consecutive elements exceeds `maxDiff`, that gap disconnects the graph and starts a new connected component. I assign a component ID to each node in one linear pass. Then each query is answered in O(1) by checking whether both nodes have the same component ID. Overall complexity is **O(n + q)** with **O(n)** extra space, which is optimal because every element and every query must be processed at least once.

---

# Key Takeaways

* Sorted array is the key observation.
* No need to construct the graph.
* Large adjacent gaps split connected components.
* Component labeling is simpler than Union-Find for this problem.
* Each query is answered in O(1).
* Overall complexity is optimal: **O(n + q)**.

---

# Common Mistakes

* Building all edges (`O(n²)`).
* Running BFS/DFS for every query.
* Comparing all pairs instead of only adjacent elements.
* Forgetting that the array is sorted.
* Starting a new component when `difference == maxDiff` (it should only start when `difference > maxDiff`).

